# `currency` Standalone Wire Specification

The `currency` codec serializes one XRPL `Currency` value without an outer
field header or VL prefix. The payload is always exactly 20 bytes (160 bits).

## JSON and wire contract

| Input / component | Wire representation | Adversarial note |
| :--- | :--- | :--- |
| `"XAH"` or `""` | 20 zero bytes | Native currency is the all-zero `Currency`; decoding normalizes it to the configured system code. The empty string is an accepted encode alias. |
| Three-character code | Characters at byte offsets 12..14 | Only the protocol's ISO-like character set is accepted. `XAH` is reserved for native currency and is not stored as three ASCII bytes. |
| 40 hexadecimal characters | The supplied 20 bytes | `noCurrency()` and the protocol's bad-currency sentinel are rejected by JSON encoding. |

Decode requires exactly 20 bytes. In a containing `STObject`, a currency field
also has its field-ID header; that header is deliberately absent from this
standalone codec.

The raw `STCurrency(SerialIter&)` constructor accepts any 160-bit value. JSON
encoding is narrower: it rejects `noCurrency()` and the case variants that
look like the native system code. Such diagnostic raw values can decode to
text that is intentionally not re-encodable.

